use std
into mover_test.amni

A test program for `physics.mover`; imports math, vec, os, physics and physics.mover. Already provided and never defined here: in namespace mover `Settings`, `Mover`, `settings()`, `new(pos, s)`, `step(world, m, wish, jump, dt)` (m by reference) and `walkable`; in namespace physics `World`, `Body`, `Quat` (x, y, z, w), `Transform` (p, q), `ShapeDef` (field `filter` with `category_bits`), `default_world_def()`, `create_world(def) World`, `destroy(world)`, `default_body_def()`, `create_body(world, def) Body` (the default body is static), `default_shape_def()`, `create_box_shape(body, shape def, half extents, frame Transform)` and `time_ns()` from os. Numbers are f32 except times. A module counter `FAILS`; `check(ok, label)` (a bool and a read-only byte slice) prints "ok " or "FAIL " and the label and counts failures. `DT` is 1/60. `r3(x)` rounds to three decimals for printing.

`yaw(a) physics::Quat` turns by a about the y axis: (0, sin(a/2), 0, cos(a/2)); `roll(a)` turns about the z axis: (0, 0, sin(a/2), cos(a/2)). `box(w, center, half, q, category)` adds a static body at the origin with one box shape of those half extents whose frame is at center with rotation q, its shape filter's category_bits set to category (1 normally). `floor(w)` is `box` with center (0, -0.5, 0), half (50, 0.5, 50), no rotation, category 1. `world() physics::World` creates a world from the default def. `run(w, m, wish, n)` steps the mover n times with that wish, no jump.

`c1()`: a world with a floor; a mover at the origin with default settings; for 180 steps with wish (1, 0, 0) record whether it was grounded at every step after the 12th and the largest |pos.y|; check grounded throughout, largest |y| under 0.01 and final horizontal speed within 2% of max_speed ("C1 flat walk"); print them. Control: same, but the settings' filter mask_bits is 0; after 180 steps check pos.y is below -1 ("C1 control: blind mover falls"). Destroy each world.

`c2()`: floor plus a wall `box` at (3, 2, 0) with half (0.1, 2, 5), no rotation, category 1; walk wish (1, 0, 0) for 180 steps, recording the largest pos.x reached; the wall face is at x 2.9; check the final x is within 0.03 below 2.9 - radius and the largest x never above 2.9 - radius + 0.005, and |vel.x| under 0.05 ("C2 wall stops"); print. Control: the same wall with category 2 and the mover's filter mask_bits 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD (everything but bit 2); check the final x is above 5 ("C2 control: unseen wall is walked through").

`c3()`: floor plus a wall at (3, 2, 0) with half (0.1, 2, 7) turned by `yaw` of π/4; its face toward the start has normal (-0.7071, 0, 0.7071) and passes through the center plus 0.1 times that normal. Walk wish (1, 0, 0) for 120 steps; record the smallest distance from the feet to that face plane (dot of pos minus the face point with the normal); the final speed along the wall direction (0.7071, 0, 0.7071) is the dot of vel with it. Check the speed is at least 0.6 × max_speed and the smallest distance at least radius - 0.01 ("C3 slides along a 45 degree wall"); print.

`step_box(top)` adds to the current world a `box` at (14.5, top / 2, 0) with half (12.5, top / 2, 5), a step from x 2 to 27. `c4()`: floor plus `step_box(0.3)`, walk wish (1, 0, 0) for 180 steps; check pos.x above 4 and |pos.y - 0.3| under 0.01 ("C4 climbs a 0.3 step"); print. Control: `step_box(0.5)` instead; check pos.x below 2 and pos.y under 0.01 ("C4 control: 0.5 step blocks").

`ramp(w, a)` adds a `box` with half (5, 0.1, 5) turned by `roll(a)` and centered at (1 + 5 cos a + 0.1 sin a, 5 sin a - 0.1 cos a, 0), so its top rises from (1, 0, 0) at slope a. `c5()`: floor plus `ramp` of π/6, walk wish (1, 0, 0) for 90 steps; check grounded, pos.y above 1.7 and |pos.y - (pos.x - 1) × tan(π/6)| under 0.05 ("C5 walks up 30 degrees"); print. Then floor plus `ramp` of π/3, 180 steps, track the largest pos.y; check it stays under 0.45 ("C5 refuses 60 degrees"). Control: the same 60 degree ramp with max_slope 1.5; check the largest y is above 1 ("C5 control: steep allowed climbs").

`c6()`: floor, mover at the origin; 12 steps with wish zero so it is standing, then one step with jump true and wish zero, then 120 steps without; track the largest pos.y and the first step after the apex where it is grounded again. With v = jump_speed and g = gravity, check the apex is within 5% of v² / (2g), it is grounded again within (2v / g + 0.2) / DT steps of the jump, and the final |pos.y| is under 0.01 ("C6 jump arc"); print the apex and the expected value.

`c7()`: floor, mover at (0, 37, 0), 300 steps with wish zero, tracking the smallest pos.y; check the smallest is at least -0.01, it ends grounded and the final |pos.y| is under 0.01 ("C7 37 m fall lands"); print.

`c8()`: a world with a floor and 100 boxes of half (0.5, 0.5, 0.5) at x and z = -17 + 3.4 × (i mod 10) and -17 + 3.4 × (i / 10), center y 0.5; 77 movers at x = -16 + 0.43 × i, z = 1.7 (between box rows, at the floor); 600 rounds where every mover steps with wish (cos(0.01 × round + i), 0, sin(0.01 × round + i)); time the whole loop with `time_ns`; microseconds per mover step = total / (77 × 600) / 1000; print it; check it is under 50 ("C8 under 50 us per mover step").

`main()`: run c1 to c8; print "mover: ok", or "mover: FAILED " and the count and exit 1.
